WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.160
Collaborative study shows drought-avoiding desert plants fared better than drought-tolerating ones during extreme heat at Uluru. This is Planetterrian Daily, Episode 226. A science deep dive on the plant work comes first, then saturated fats at the synapse and antibodies that block interluchin 10. A record-breaking hot drought from 2018 to 2019 caused widespread plant mortality in Uluru, Katachuta National Park, according to work covered by Fizz.org.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.680
Researchers surveyed nine species over a mild spring and an extreme summer, measuring

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.040
leaf traits, water content, and carbon nitrogen ratios.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.600
Anangu traditional owners co-developed the research aims, selected the species, conducted

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.320
the fieldwork, and interpreted the results with plant physiologists.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.900
Winari, Chanp and Kirkara were among the culturally significant plants chosen because they supply

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.020
food, habitat, and tool materials.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:01:00.560
A nangu knowledge of country, fire, and landscape change was woven directly into the western

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.100
plant physiology framework throughout the project.

00:01:03.866 --> 00:01:08.246
Something most people get wrong is the idea that desert plants have full-proof defenses

00:01:08.246 --> 00:01:11.746
against heat and drought, simply because they live in arid zones.

00:01:12.286 --> 00:01:17.426
Plants that keep growing during dry spells maintain carbon uptake, but risk running out of water

00:01:17.426 --> 00:01:18.906
when conditions intensify.

00:01:19.466 --> 00:01:24.266
Plants that shut down growth conserve water, yet they can exhaust stored reserves if the

00:01:24.266 --> 00:01:29.026
drought last too long. The team found that maintaining stable water levels inside leaves

00:01:29.026 --> 00:01:34.026
was the single best predictor of survival after comparing multiple physiological traits,

00:01:34.546 --> 00:01:37.986
including carbon nitrogen ratios and overall leaf morphology.

00:01:38.626 --> 00:01:43.766
Leaf relative water content emerged as that strongest statistical predictor. Plants that

00:01:43.766 --> 00:01:48.786
experience large reductions in leaf water content suffered the most severe dieback, regardless

00:01:48.786 --> 00:01:53.866
of their primary drought strategy. Species that lost this internal water balance suffered

00:01:53.866 --> 00:01:58.206
the highest dieback once temperatures and water deficits reached the levels recorded in

00:01:58.206 --> 00:02:00.246
2018 to 2019.

00:02:01.126 --> 00:02:05.626
Neither drought tolerance nor drought avoidance conferred reliable protection at those combined

00:02:05.626 --> 00:02:06.086
extremes.

00:02:06.766 --> 00:02:11.086
One practical step that follows is supporting community nurseries that propagate the more

00:02:11.086 --> 00:02:16.026
resilient drought avoiding species, while also advancing cultural burning practices that

00:02:16.026 --> 00:02:17.726
reduce fuel loads and competition.

00:02:18.466 --> 00:02:23.386
Future work will test whether restoring traditional fire regimes can reduce competition and improve

00:02:23.386 --> 00:02:27.306
water availability for those drought-avoiding species identified in the survey.

00:02:27.946 --> 00:02:32.406
Continued monitoring will show whether these local actions can buffer the next extreme drought,

00:02:32.646 --> 00:02:37.226
or whether only larger reductions in global emissions will keep the current range of desert plants

00:02:37.226 --> 00:02:42.586
viable. The researchers note that sustained reductions in anthropogenic climate forcing will

00:02:42.586 --> 00:02:47.426
ultimately determine whether these desert ecosystems retain their current diversity of keystone

00:02:47.426 --> 00:02:53.374
plants. University of Queensland researchers found that saturated free fatty acids contribute

00:02:53.374 --> 00:02:59.254
to both energy production and signaling required for synaptic plasticity in work circulating via

00:02:59.254 --> 00:03:04.934
science subreddit. The team challenged prior assumptions about brain bioenergetics by demonstrating

00:03:04.934 --> 00:03:10.954
that these lipids directly support neuronal communication processes. Experiments tracked how the fatty

00:03:10.954 --> 00:03:16.614
acids participate in the metabolic and signaling pathways that enable synapses to strengthen or weaken.

00:03:17.234 --> 00:03:22.614
The work focused on how these molecules supply ATP while also acting as signaling intermediates

00:03:22.614 --> 00:03:27.654
at the synapse. This dual role was measured across conditions that alter synaptic strength

00:03:27.654 --> 00:03:33.496
in living tissue. Earlier models treated saturated fatty acids mainly as structural components

00:03:33.496 --> 00:03:38.576
or secondary metabolites, and the new measurements overturned those models that downplayed them

00:03:38.576 --> 00:03:43.356
in brain energy dynamics. The measurements linked their abundance directly to the metabolic

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:49.376
demands of long-term potentiation and long-term depression. The experiments isolated the contribution

00:03:49.376 --> 00:03:54.796
of saturated species from other lipid classes to confirm their dual-energetic and messenger roles.

00:03:55.276 --> 00:03:59.716
That separation clarifies why previous studies that grouped all fatty acids together

00:03:59.716 --> 00:04:05.196
may have missed the functional specificity now observed. Because synaptic plasticity underpins

00:04:05.196 --> 00:04:10.696
learning and memory, the finding reframes how shifts in fatty acid profiles could influence cognitive

00:04:10.696 --> 00:04:17.918
flexibility. Oxford, Newcastle, and Cambridge researchers examined more than 4,900 people with

00:04:17.918 --> 00:04:24.258
inflammatory bowel disease and found roughly 3.5% carried antibodies that neutralize interleukin

00:04:24.258 --> 00:04:26.338
10, according to science daily.

00:04:26.918 --> 00:04:31.878
These auto-antibodies disable a key regulator of inflammation and associate strongly with

00:04:31.878 --> 00:04:37.958
the HLA-DRB-1-103-AM genetic variant, previously linked to severe disease.

00:04:38.618 --> 00:04:41.198
The same antibodies were absent in healthy controls.

00:04:41.798 --> 00:04:46.198
The antibodies were detected in both Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis patients.

00:04:46.198 --> 00:04:51.358
The discovery clarifies how one of the strongest known genetic risk factors for inflammatory

00:04:51.358 --> 00:04:54.158
bowel disease produces intestinal inflammation.

00:04:54.838 --> 00:05:00.018
Researchers estimate that 15,000 to 20,000 people in the United Kingdom alone may carry

00:05:00.018 --> 00:05:01.598
these neutralizing antibodies.

00:05:02.459 --> 00:05:08.379
Royal Botanic Gardens' Q researchers analyzed historic specimens and DNA sequences to distinguish

00:05:08.379 --> 00:05:15.459
Ganoderma lucidum, described in 1781 from Ganoderma Lingji used in traditional East Asian medicine.

00:05:15.459 --> 00:05:21.219
In a Fizz.org report, the two species diverged at least 16 million years ago.

00:05:21.959 --> 00:05:29.039
Conventional DNA barcoding correctly identified only 149 of 1130 public sequences assigned

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:30.359
to Ganoderma lucidum.

00:05:30.819 --> 00:05:35.599
A mislabeled hazel tree specimen collected near London supplied the first usable genetic

00:05:35.599 --> 00:05:38.699
material matching the original 1781 description.

00:05:39.239 --> 00:05:45.299
The study shows that accurate species identification requires multiple genomic fragments plus evolutionary

00:05:45.299 --> 00:05:49.799
modeling rather than single barcode approaches. The team proposes calling

00:05:49.799 --> 00:05:54.619
Ganoderma lucidum false-lingi to reduce misidentification in pharmacological

00:05:54.619 --> 00:05:59.670
research and consumer products. Spiders displayed distinct sleep stages that

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.950
parallel patterns seen in other animals in behavioral observations summarized on

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:08.190
the science subreddit. The study recorded periods of reduced responsiveness

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:12.730
followed by a second phase with different physiological markers. The two phases

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:17.070
were distinguished by changes in arousal threshold and movement patterns during rest.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:21.950
Researchers observed these stages across multiple individuals under controlled laboratory

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:26.850
conditions. The findings add spiders to the list of invertebrates displaying organized

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:32.681
sleep architecture. Functional imaging examined how individuals with higher narcissistic traits

00:06:32.681 --> 00:06:38.161
process social situations that require empathy. The work focused on brain responses during

00:06:38.161 --> 00:06:43.581
complex interpersonal scenarios rather than isolated tasks. Participants with elevated

00:06:43.581 --> 00:06:48.221
grandiose narcissism scores showed distinct activation patterns when interpreting others'

00:06:48.461 --> 00:06:53.621
mental states in dynamic social contexts. Results highlight reduced engagement in networks

00:06:53.621 --> 00:06:59.121
typically linked to perspective taking. The study design emphasized ecologically valid stimuli

00:06:59.121 --> 00:07:05.035
to capture real-world social cognition demands. Face-to-face interactions with friends emerged

00:07:05.035 --> 00:07:08.595
as the strongest predictor of mental health outcomes among college students.

00:07:09.035 --> 00:07:14.535
A study of nearly 80,000 students found that exercise, sleep duration, and alcohol avoidance

00:07:14.535 --> 00:07:19.475
showed weaker associations once social contact was accounted for. The pattern held across

00:07:19.475 --> 00:07:25.015
multiple campuses and demographic groups. Researchers collected data through repeated surveys that

00:07:25.015 --> 00:07:30.255
captured both social behavior and self-reported well-being measures. The large sample size

00:07:30.255 --> 00:07:34.855
is allowed statistical separation of social contact effects from other lifestyle variables,

00:07:35.787 --> 00:07:40.487
leading researchers in the well-being field, reviewing that same ranking of social connection,

00:07:41.047 --> 00:07:45.147
report prioritizing time with loved ones after looking at accumulated evidence.

00:07:45.567 --> 00:07:50.147
One investigator stated that relationships with other people represent the single strongest

00:07:50.147 --> 00:07:51.327
contributor to happiness.

00:07:52.047 --> 00:07:56.667
Population-level data consistently plays social connections above income or achievement

00:07:56.667 --> 00:07:58.167
metrics in predictive models.

00:07:58.167 --> 00:08:03.147
The survey captured how prominent happiness scientists personally apply their own research

00:08:03.147 --> 00:08:04.507
findings in daily life.

00:08:04.907 --> 00:08:09.587
Multiple independent data sets converged on the same ranking of relationship quality as

00:08:09.587 --> 00:08:10.727
the dominant predictor.

00:08:11.565 --> 00:08:16.705
Sixteen years of monitoring data show that changes in freshwater inflow reshape algal species

00:08:16.705 --> 00:08:19.985
composition in South Australia's Kuran Lagoon system.

00:08:20.745 --> 00:08:25.125
Shifts in salinity and nutrient delivery track directly with river management decisions.

00:08:25.125 --> 00:08:30.325
Continuous sampling captured both seasonal cycles and multi-year trends linked to upstream

00:08:30.325 --> 00:08:31.145
water releases.

00:08:31.645 --> 00:08:36.465
The long-term record supplies a baseline for testing adaptation strategies under changing

00:08:36.465 --> 00:08:37.405
climate conditions.

00:08:37.925 --> 00:08:42.865
The data set now supports modeling of how altered flow regimes may affect primary producers

00:08:42.865 --> 00:08:44.305
and dependent food webs.

00:08:45.259 --> 00:08:49.979
Field measurements of energy expenditure and wild panamanian bats demonstrate how individuals

00:08:49.979 --> 00:08:53.359
operate near their physiological limits during nightly foraging.

00:08:53.359 --> 00:08:58.459
Direct observations of flight duration and foraging intensity were paired with physiological

00:08:58.459 --> 00:09:01.159
sampling to quantify daily energy budgets.

00:09:01.839 --> 00:09:06.499
The data links specific activity budgets to population persistence under habitat pressure.

00:09:07.139 --> 00:09:11.899
The study highlights that bats routinely approach their maximum sustainable metabolic rate when

00:09:11.899 --> 00:09:12.759
securing food.

00:09:13.299 --> 00:09:18.179
Researchers propose incorporating these energetic ceilings into future conservation assessments.

00:09:18.179 --> 00:09:24.274
In brief, neuroscientists describe how descending pathways from the brainstem cross to control

00:09:24.274 --> 00:09:28.914
opposite sides of the body, producing the familiar right brain left body pattern.

00:09:29.614 --> 00:09:34.154
Political scientists and psychologists use platform data to quantify how expressions

00:09:34.154 --> 00:09:36.714
of support evolve across prolonged conflicts.

00:09:37.254 --> 00:09:42.694
Before we go, continued monitoring at Uluru will test whether restoring traditional fire regimes

00:09:42.694 --> 00:09:45.774
and community nurseries can buffer the next extreme drought.

00:09:45.774 --> 00:09:50.634
